## Test Smell Audit — Academic Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10  
**Integration calibration applied:** Class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]` — Mystery Guest and Eager Test thresholds relaxed per calibration rules.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Total    | 4           | 4              |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell 1)

**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 95)  
**Problem:** `if/else` branch means only one assertion path ever runs — whichever type the system returns, the other branch is untested.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

```csharp
// ✅ Fix — split into two deterministic tests
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com") as PremiumUser;
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is permanently untested — a bug hiding there will never be caught.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test (Smell 3)

**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)  
**Problem:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` introduces 3 seconds of wall-clock delay and is non-deterministic — on a slow CI runner it may not be enough; locally it wastes time.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

```csharp
// ✅ Fix — poll with timeout
var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    if (DateTime.UtcNow > deadline)
        Assert.Fail("Notification was not sent within 5 s.");
    await Task.Delay(50);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI; unnecessary 3 s added to every suite run.

> **Integration calibration note:** Even for integration tests, fixed sleeps are a smell — they trade reliability for simplicity. Polling/event-waits are the accepted alternative.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell 4)

**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)  
**Problem:** Inserts 100 rows but never asserts anything. If `Insert` silently drops records, this test still passes.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertion
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
```

```csharp
// ✅ Fix — verify the outcome
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

var users = _repository.ListAll();
Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage reports show green but no behavior is actually verified.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Catalog)

**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)  
**Problem:** 4 assertions with no message parameters. When one fails in CI, the output is `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>.` — which property was null?

```csharp
// ❌ Current
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

```csharp
// ✅ Fix — add diagnostic messages
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis; developers must read source to locate the broken assertion.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-structured — clear arrange/act/assert, single responsibility, self-documenting counts:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused workflow, clear assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single action, single assert on absence |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is self-documenting (3 items just inserted) — not a magic number |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Legitimate concurrency integration test; count `10` is contextual |

The in-memory SQLite pattern (`Data Source=:memory:`) used in setup is **not** a Mystery Guest — it's a hermetic, test-scoped resource. Good choice.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | ~1 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into 2 typed tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add message strings to assertions | ~2 min |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests have formal smells, but none of the smells are catastrophic. Fix #1 and #2 first — they directly undermine the suite's defect-detection ability. The remaining 4 tests are solid integration tests.
